"""Knowledge pack: backtesting, research methodology, data and machine learning for trading."""

from __future__ import annotations

DOMAIN = "Backtesting, research methodology and financial machine learning"

LESSONS: list[tuple[str, str]] = [
    ("Event-driven versus vectorized backtesters", "A vectorized backtester computes signals and returns as whole-array operations in pandas or numpy: shift the signal by one bar, multiply by returns, cumulate. It is fast, ideal for scanning thousands of parameter combinations, but it cannot naturally model path-dependent logic such as stop losses, partial fills, margin calls or order queues, and it invites look-ahead bias because the whole series is in memory. An event-driven backtester replays bars or ticks one at a time through the same handlers that live trading uses, so orders, fills, cash and positions evolve realistically and the code can be reused in production. It is ten to a thousand times slower and harder to debug. Good practice is vectorized research for idea screening, then event-driven confirmation with transaction costs and slippage before any capital is committed."),
    ("Backtesting frameworks and their trade-offs", "Backtrader is a mature event-driven Python framework with broad broker integrations but slow loops and little active maintenance. Backtesting.py is small and readable, fine for single-instrument rule strategies with a built-in optimizer and heatmaps, but not for portfolios. Vectorbt runs numba-accelerated vectorized simulations over thousands of parameter sets in seconds and is the best tool for sensitivity analysis, at the cost of awkward path-dependent logic. Zipline powered Quantopian and models point-in-time universes well, but its data bundles are painful to maintain since Quantopian closed. QuantConnect Lean is a C# engine with Python algorithms, institutional data and cloud execution, heavier and partly tied to its platform. NautilusTrader is a Rust-cored event engine built for latency-sensitive live trading with identical code in backtest. Choose by asset class, whether the code must go live, and how many variants you need to test."),
    ("Data sources and their quality issues", "yfinance scrapes Yahoo Finance: free and convenient, but it breaks when Yahoo changes its site, has silent gaps, back-adjusts history inconsistently, and suits prototyping only. Polygon sells licensed US equities and options tick data with adjustments and reference data. Alpaca gives free IEX-only minute bars, which miss most consolidated volume, plus paid SIP data. Databento sells normalized, precisely timestamped exchange data with explicit publisher and schema metadata, the cleanest option for microstructure research. The Binance API gives free crypto klines and trades, but history begins at listing and delisted pairs vanish, a survivorship bias trap. Kaggle datasets are convenient but often unadjusted, undocumented and stale. Always record the vendor, download date and adjustment method with any dataset, because a backtest is only as good as the data it consumed."),
    ("Adjusted versus unadjusted prices and dividends", "Vendors adjust historical prices backward for splits and often for dividends: a 2-for-1 split halves every earlier price, and a dividend scales earlier prices down by the payout fraction. Backtesting returns needs total-return adjusted series, otherwise a dividend-paying stock shows a fake drop on each ex-date and long strategies understate returns by the dividend yield, around 2% a year for the S&P 500 and far more for REITs and utilities. But signals that reference price levels, such as dollar thresholds, round numbers, option strikes or share counts, must use unadjusted prices, because a trader in 2010 never saw the adjusted values. Adjusted series also change every time a new dividend is paid, so stored backtest results drift. Keep raw prices plus an adjustment factor column and apply adjustments at query time."),
    ("Point-in-time fundamentals", "Fundamental data is restated, corrected and reported with a lag. A quarter ending March 31 is filed in May and may be restated in the annual report, yet most databases overwrite the original with the final figure and stamp it with the period end date. A backtest that ranks stocks on that value in April is using numbers nobody had until May, a look-ahead bias that inflates value and quality strategies noticeably. Point-in-time databases such as Compustat snapshots, Sharadar and SEC EDGAR filing dates record when each value became public and what it said at the time. Without them, lag fundamentals by at least 90 days for quarterly and 180 days for annual data, then check whether the anomaly survives; many published fundamental effects shrink once this is done."),
    ("Delisting returns", "When a stock is delisted for bankruptcy the final return is often minus 30% to minus 100%, but many datasets simply stop the series at the last traded price or drop the ticker entirely. Shumway (1997) showed that ignoring delisting returns overstates small-cap and value returns materially, and later work found the same for momentum losers and low-price stocks. Mergers produce a positive delisting return that also belongs in the record. CRSP stores delisting returns explicitly; free sources rarely do. A practical fix is to assign a conservative delisting return, minus 30% for performance-related delistings and the deal price for mergers, and to keep dead tickers in the universe file. This is the mechanism behind survivorship bias, and it is largest in exactly the strategies that look best on clean data."),
    ("Timezone and session alignment bugs", "Timestamps are the most common source of silent backtest errors. A bar stamp can mean the open or the close of the interval, and vendors differ. Daily bars for futures and forex begin at different hours than equities, so aligning an S&P future with a stock on the same calendar date can pair a bar containing the next morning with one that closed the previous afternoon. Crypto runs on UTC and never closes; US equities switch between EST and EDT; Europe and the US shift daylight saving on different weeks, so a fixed hour offset breaks twice a year. Store everything in UTC with an explicit exchange calendar such as exchange_calendars, and test alignment by checking that a known event, such as an FOMC announcement, lands in the right bar. Any strategy that mixes markets or intraday data needs this audit."),
    ("The look-ahead bias catalogue", "Look-ahead bias is any use of information not yet available at decision time. The recurring forms: entering at the close of the bar that generated the signal, when a close-based signal can only be acted on at the next bar; computing indicators, normalizations or z-scores over the whole series so today's value depends on future means; centered moving averages and full-sample regressions; using rebalance dates or constituents from a future index composition; scaling by realized volatility measured over a window that includes the current return; filling missing values with future observations; using the day's high to decide an intraday entry; and reading fundamentals as of period end rather than filing date. Each alone can double a Sharpe ratio. The cure is an event-driven backtest or a strict one-bar shift of every feature, plus a lag audit before any result is trusted."),
    ("Fill assumptions and how much they change results", "How a backtest converts a signal into a fill matters as much as the signal. Filling at the same bar's close is the optimistic default and, for close-based signals, is look-ahead bias. Filling at the next open is realistic for daily systems but exposes overnight gaps, which shave a fraction of a percent per year from momentum strategies and much more from short-term mean reversion. Assuming a limit order fills whenever price touches the limit is wrong: at the touch you are at the back of the queue, so an honest rule requires price to trade through the limit. When a bar hits both the stop and the target, assume the worse outcome. Moving from close fills to next-open fills with realistic limit logic often turns a Sharpe ratio of 2 into something near zero, so test at least two fill assumptions on every strategy."),
    ("Realistic cost models by asset class", "Transaction costs must be modeled per asset class rather than as a flat fee. Large-cap US equities cost roughly 1-3 basis points in spread plus market impact that grows with the square root of participation rate; small caps cost 20-100 basis points and can be untradeable in size. Index futures such as ES cost a quarter tick, around 0.6 basis points, plus a small commission, which is why futures dominate fast systematic strategies. Spot forex majors cost 0.5-2 pips at retail brokers, more around news. Crypto on major exchanges costs 5-10 basis points in taker fees per side plus spread, so a strategy that trades daily pays 40% or more a year. Options carry spreads of 5-10% of premium. Add borrow fees, funding rates and financing. If edge per trade is under twice the round-trip cost, the strategy will not survive slippage."),
    ("Parameter sensitivity analysis and heatmaps", "A robust strategy performs reasonably across a neighborhood of parameters, not at a single spike. The standard test is a grid over the two most important parameters, such as lookback and threshold, plotted as a heatmap of Sharpe ratio or return. A broad plateau of similar results suggests a real effect; an isolated peak surrounded by losses is overfitting, and the peak should be treated as a lucky draw. Report the median of the plateau rather than the best cell and choose a parameter from its middle. Vectorbt and backtesting.py generate such grids cheaply. Also vary what is not usually optimized: start date, universe, rebalance day and fill assumption, because sensitivity there reveals fragility the main parameters hide. If shifting the start date by a month changes the ranking of parameters, the sample is too short."),
    ("The deflated Sharpe ratio and multiple testing", "When many strategy variants are tried and the best is reported, the expected maximum Sharpe ratio under the null of no skill grows with the number of trials, roughly with the square root of twice the log of the count. Bailey and Lopez de Prado's deflated Sharpe ratio (2014) turns this into a test: given the number of independent trials, the variance of Sharpe ratios across them, the track record length, and the skewness and kurtosis of returns, it computes the probability that the observed Sharpe ratio exceeds what luck alone would produce. Ten independent trials over a few years of daily data typically require an observed annualized Sharpe of about 1.5 before it is credible, and a hundred trials push that higher. Honest use requires recording every trial, including abandoned ones, which is a research discipline rather than a formula."),
    ("Probability of backtest overfitting: PBO and CSCV", "Bailey, Borwein, Lopez de Prado and Zhu proposed combinatorially symmetric cross-validation (CSCV) to estimate the probability of backtest overfitting. The returns matrix of all tried variants is cut into S blocks, and every combination of half the blocks forms a training set with the other half as test. In each split the best in-sample variant is chosen and its out-of-sample rank among all variants is recorded. PBO is the fraction of splits where that in-sample winner ranks below the median out of sample. A PBO under about 10% suggests the selection process finds something real; a PBO near 50% means selection is no better than a coin flip. The method needs the full set of trials, not only the survivor, and its weaknesses are highly correlated variants and short samples. It should be a routine check after any optimization."),
    ("Purged and embargoed cross-validation", "Ordinary k-fold cross-validation assumes independent samples, but financial labels overlap: a label formed today may depend on prices over the next twenty days, so a training sample can share its outcome window with a test sample and leak. Lopez de Prado's purged cross-validation removes from the training set any observation whose label period overlaps a test observation's label period. Embargo then drops a further slice of training data immediately after each test fold, typically about 1% of the sample, to absorb serial correlation in features. Without these steps, models on daily data with multi-day labels routinely show cross-validated accuracy several points above their true level, and feature importance becomes unreliable. Purging shrinks the training set, so a few long test folds beat many small ones. This is the minimum standard for evaluating any machine learning model on financial time series."),
    ("Combinatorial purged cross-validation", "Walk-forward gives a single out-of-sample path, so its performance estimate is one noisy draw. Combinatorial purged cross-validation (CPCV) splits the data into N groups and tests on every combination of k groups, purging and embargoing around each, so that many distinct backtest paths are assembled from the test folds. With N of 6 and k of 2 there are 15 combinations producing 5 complete paths, each a full-length out-of-sample equity curve. The distribution of Sharpe ratios across paths shows how much a result depends on the particular sequence of history, which walk-forward cannot reveal. It is computationally heavier and trains on non-chronological data, so it answers whether the model generalizes rather than whether it would have made money in sequence. Use CPCV for model selection and a final walk-forward for the deployment estimate."),
    ("Walk-forward efficiency ratio", "Robert Pardo defined walk-forward efficiency as the annualized out-of-sample return of the stitched walk-forward test divided by the annualized in-sample return of the optimizations. An efficiency of 50-60% or better is the conventional bar for a strategy likely to hold up; below 30% the optimization mostly fit noise. The ratio is sensitive to window lengths: short in-sample windows adapt quickly but overfit, long ones stabilize but lag regime changes, so measure it across several choices such as 2, 3 and 5 years in-sample with 6 to 12 months out. A related check is the fraction of walk-forward steps that are profitable; a strategy that makes all its money in one step is not robust. Walk-forward efficiency is a heuristic, not a statistical test, and it says nothing about how many strategies were tried before this one."),
    ("Minimum track record length", "Bailey and Lopez de Prado's minimum track record length answers how long a strategy must be observed before its Sharpe ratio can be distinguished from a benchmark at a given confidence. It depends on the observed Sharpe, the target Sharpe, return skewness and kurtosis, and sampling frequency. For an annualized Sharpe of 1 against a benchmark of zero at 95% confidence with roughly normal daily returns, about 2.7 years are needed; a Sharpe of 0.5 needs more than 10 years, and negative skew stretches these figures further. Combined with the probabilistic Sharpe ratio, the probability that the true Sharpe exceeds the benchmark, it tells a trader whether a live track record is informative yet. Most six-month live records carry almost no statistical weight, which is why paper trading periods exist to catch bugs rather than to prove edge."),
    ("Bootstrapping and Monte Carlo of trade sequences", "A single equity curve is one realization of a random process. Resampling the sequence of trade returns with replacement, or shuffling their order, and rebuilding thousands of equity curves gives distributions of maximum drawdown, time to recovery and terminal wealth that the original curve hides. Typical findings: the median resampled max drawdown is 1.5 to 2 times the backtested one, and roughly 5% of paths show drawdowns beyond any historical experience. Block bootstrap, which resamples contiguous runs of trades, preserves autocorrelation and loss clustering; simple shuffling destroys them and understates risk. Monte Carlo can also perturb entry prices, skip random trades or add cost noise to test fragility. Resampling cannot invent regimes that never occurred in the sample, so it measures sequence risk, not model risk. Use the results to set position size and drawdown limits."),
    ("White's reality check and Hansen's SPA test", "White's reality check (2000) asks whether the best of many strategies beats a benchmark after accounting for the search itself. It bootstraps the joint distribution of all strategies' excess returns with a stationary block bootstrap and compares the observed best performance with the distribution of the best performance under the null that none has an edge. Hansen's superior predictive ability test (2005) refines this by standardizing each strategy and discounting hopelessly bad ones that would otherwise make the test too lenient. In plain terms, both ask: if I tried this many rules on noise, how often would the winner look this good? Sullivan, Timmermann and White applied it to nearly 8,000 technical trading rules on the Dow and found nothing significant after the correction. These tests need the full population of rules tested, which is the usual practical obstacle."),
    ("Feature engineering for financial machine learning", "Raw prices are non-stationary, so features must be transformed into quantities with stable distributions. Standard features include log returns over several horizons, realized volatility and its ratio across windows, distance from moving averages in volatility units, volume relative to its average, and cross-sectional ranks that neutralize market moves. Microstructure features such as order flow imbalance, bid-ask spread, Kyle's lambda, VPIN and trade-size distributions carry most of the predictive content at intraday horizons. Fractional differentiation, from Lopez de Prado, differences a series by a fractional order d, often 0.3 to 0.5, so it passes a stationarity test while keeping memory that integer differencing destroys. Scale features with statistics from the training window only, winsorize outliers, and lag everything so it is known at decision time. More features rarely help; signal-to-noise is low and each addition raises the chance of spurious fits."),
    ("Labeling: triple barrier and meta-labeling", "Fixed-horizon labels, such as the sign of the return over the next ten days, ignore the path and the trader's stops. The triple barrier method labels each observation by which of three barriers is hit first: an upper profit-taking barrier, a lower stop-loss barrier, both set as multiples of current volatility, or a vertical time limit. This matches how trades actually end and ties labels to volatility. Meta-labeling separates side from size: a primary model or simple rule decides long or short, and a secondary classifier predicts whether that primary signal will be profitable, with its probability used to size the bet or skip it. Meta-labeling improves precision without touching recall, tolerates a weak primary model, and yields interpretable outputs. Sample weights should reflect the uniqueness of overlapping labels, since concurrent labels are not independent observations."),
    ("Tree models versus neural networks on financial data", "On tabular financial features at daily or lower frequency, gradient boosted trees such as LightGBM, XGBoost and CatBoost, and random forests, generally match or beat neural networks, need less tuning, handle missing values and interactions, and retrain fast enough for walk-forward use. Gu, Kelly and Xiu (2020) found that trees and shallow neural networks both improved on linear models for cross-sectional stock returns, with monthly out-of-sample R-squared below 1%, which is the honest scale of predictability. Neural networks win when the input is a high-dimensional raw sequence, such as order book snapshots or text, and when millions of samples exist. Regularize aggressively: shallow trees, strong shrinkage, few features, and validation that respects time. A boosted tree reporting 60% directional accuracy on daily equity returns is almost always leaking data."),
    ("LSTMs and transformers for price prediction", "Papers claiming that LSTM or transformer models forecast prices with high accuracy are common, and most fail replication. Typical flaws: predicting the level rather than the return, so the model learns to output yesterday's price and scores a tiny error; normalizing with the full sample; testing on a single stock in a single bull market; and ignoring transaction costs. Evaluated properly on returns with purged validation, sequence models on daily bars reach directional accuracy of 50-53%, not reliably better than boosted trees on engineered features. They do add value at high frequency with deep limit order book data, as in DeepLOB, where millions of samples exist, and in encoding text and events. Transformers help with multi-horizon forecasts of volatility and volume, which are far more predictable than direction. Treat any published accuracy above 60% on daily direction as a bug until reproduced."),
    ("Reinforcement learning for trading", "Reinforcement learning frames trading as an agent choosing positions to maximize cumulative reward, learning from simulated interaction. The appeal is that it optimizes the trading objective directly, including costs and position limits, instead of a prediction proxy. The honest evidence is thin: financial environments are non-stationary, rewards are extremely noisy, and an agent trained on a few thousand days sees a tiny fraction of possible states, so results across papers are fragile and rarely replicate out of sample. Published successes on benchmarks such as FinRL often reflect a bull market or a favorable reward design. The sound applications are optimal execution and market making, where the environment is closer to stationary, simulators exist, and rewards are dense. For signal generation, a supervised model with a rule-based sizing layer usually performs as well with far less variance."),
    ("LLMs and news sentiment features", "Large language models turn headlines, filings and social posts into sentiment scores or event labels that become features. FinBERT and general LLMs classify tone better than dictionary methods, and zero-shot prompting of GPT-class models has shown modest predictive power for next-day returns on headlines (Lopez-Lira and Tang, 2023), concentrated in small stocks and decaying within a day or two. The pitfalls: a model whose pretraining data covers the test period can recall outcomes, a subtle look-ahead bias; news timestamps are often later than the market reaction; and sentiment from the same wire services is crowded. Costs and latency consume most of the short-lived edge. LLMs are more useful for structuring unstructured data, such as entities, earnings call themes and supply-chain links, than for forecasting directly. Always compare against a simple keyword baseline and audit the timestamp pipeline first."),
    ("Feature importance and leakage detection", "Feature importance serves two purposes: understanding what drives a model, and catching leaks. Mean decrease impurity from tree models is biased toward high-cardinality and correlated features; permutation importance computed on purged out-of-sample folds is more reliable, and Shapley values give per-prediction attribution. Clustered feature importance groups correlated features first so substitution effects do not split their importance. The leakage test is simple: a feature whose importance towers over every other, or a model whose out-of-sample accuracy is implausibly high, almost always contains future information, an unlagged target transform, or an identifier that proxies for time. Shuffle the target and retrain; if importance or accuracy stays high, there is a bug. Train on data ending a year earlier and see whether the feature still matters. These checks take minutes and prevent most fake results."),
    ("Non-stationarity and concept drift", "Financial relationships change: volatility regimes shift, participants adapt, regulation alters microstructure, and an exploited anomaly stops working. For machine learning this is concept drift: the mapping from features to outcomes moves, so a model fit to 2005-2015 may be inverted after 2020. Symptoms are a gradual decline in out-of-sample accuracy, feature distributions drifting outside the training range, and calibration failure where predicted probabilities no longer match outcomes. Defenses include rolling retraining, exponentially weighted training samples, features expressed as ranks or volatility units rather than raw levels, monitoring the population stability index of inputs, and explicit change-point detection. No fix restores a dead edge; the realistic goal is to detect drift early enough to cut size. Most strategies that die do so from drift, not from a single event."),
    ("Regime-aware validation", "A backtest averaged over a long sample can hide that all the profit came from one regime. Regime-aware validation splits results by state: bull and bear markets, high and low VIX, rising and falling rates, and before and after structural changes such as decimalization in 2001, the 2008 crisis or the 2020 crash. Report Sharpe ratio, drawdown and hit rate within each regime with the number of observations, since a regime of 30 days proves nothing. Hold out at least one full cycle; a test window containing only a bull market is not a test. Hidden Markov models or volatility thresholds can define regimes, but define them before looking at results, otherwise you pick regimes that flatter the strategy. A strategy that works in one regime is not worthless, but it must be sized or switched off by a regime filter that is itself validated out of sample."),
    ("Live versus backtest divergence and how to diagnose it", "Live results almost always trail the backtest. The usual sources, roughly by size: transaction costs and slippage above the model; fills the backtest assumed and the market never provided; live feed timestamps or adjustments that differ from the historical vendor; live code computing an indicator differently or on a different bar boundary; latency between signal and order; and plain edge decay. Diagnose by logging every live signal, order, fill and the data snapshot that produced it, then re-running the backtest over the same live period and comparing trade by trade rather than by aggregate return. A consistent shortfall of a few basis points per trade points to costs; a different trade list points to code or data. Expect live Sharpe ratio to be about half the backtest even when everything is right."),
    ("Paper trading periods before going live", "A paper trading period runs the full production stack against live data without capital. Its purpose is to find integration bugs: wrong symbols, timezone errors, orders sized in the wrong units, reconnect failures, signals computed on incomplete bars, and vendor data that differs from the backtest source. It cannot prove edge, because a few months carry no statistical weight and simulated fills are optimistic, especially for limit orders and thin markets. A reasonable protocol is one to three months of paper trading with every signal logged and compared trade by trade against a backtest of the same period, then a small-capital live phase at 10-25% of intended size for several more months before scaling. Treat any unexplained trade-list mismatch as a blocker. Brokers such as Alpaca and Interactive Brokers provide paper accounts, and their fill simulation is generous."),
    ("Research reproducibility and experiment tracking", "A result that cannot be regenerated from code, data version and configuration is not a result. Fix random seeds, pin library versions, and store the exact data snapshot or its hash, since vendors revise history. Log every experiment, including failures, with parameters, sample period, universe, cost model and metrics, using MLflow, Weights and Biases or a simple append-only table. This log is also what makes the deflated Sharpe ratio and PBO computable, because both need the number of trials. Keep exploration in notebooks but move anything that feeds a decision into a tested package, and re-run the full pipeline from raw data on a clean machine before believing a result. Many spectacular findings vanish at this step, which is the point. Reproducibility is cheap insurance against the most common research failure, which is fooling yourself."),
    ("The research pipeline from idea to deployment", "A disciplined pipeline moves an idea through stages with an explicit gate at each. Begin with a hypothesis that has an economic reason and a specific prediction. Check data availability and build a clean point-in-time dataset. Run a fast vectorized backtest with conservative costs to see whether the effect exists at all; most ideas die here. Then test robustness: parameter heatmaps, subperiods, regimes, universes and fill assumptions. Estimate capacity and turnover. Confirm with an event-driven backtest that shares code with production. Compute the deflated Sharpe ratio and PBO from the full trial log. Paper trade, go live small, then scale. Document each stage so a colleague can audit it. Lopez de Prado argues for splitting these roles across specialists so that no one person both hunts for a pattern and judges it, which is the structural cure for overfitting."),
    ("Strategy monitoring and kill criteria", "Before a strategy goes live, define what would make you stop it, because deciding during a drawdown is emotional. Common kill criteria: a drawdown deeper than 1.5 to 2 times the backtested maximum, a rolling six-month Sharpe ratio below a threshold consistent with the strategy's expected distribution, trade count or turnover far from expectation, live slippage persistently above the model, or a correlation break with the signals it should track. Monitor daily: realized versus expected returns, fill quality, data freshness, exposure limits and error logs. Use the probabilistic Sharpe ratio or a CUSUM test on excess returns to separate an unlucky run from decay, remembering that a healthy Sharpe 1 strategy has a losing year about one time in six. Reduce size at the first trigger and stop at the second; restart only after an explained cause."),
    ("Backtest results that are too good to be true", "Some patterns almost always signal a bug rather than an edge. A Sharpe ratio above 3 on daily bars in a liquid market. A win rate over 70% with an average winner larger than the average loser. An equity curve that is a straight line with no drawdown longer than a few weeks. Returns unchanged when transaction costs are doubled. Performance that improves as the holding period shrinks toward one bar, the fingerprint of same-bar fills. A strategy that works on every asset with the same parameters. Profits concentrated in a handful of trades or a single year. Any accuracy above 60% predicting daily direction. When one of these appears, search for look-ahead bias, survivorship bias, fill assumptions and data errors before celebrating. Experienced quants treat an outstanding result as a bug report, and they are usually right."),
    ("Decay of published anomalies: McLean and Pontiff", "McLean and Pontiff (2016) re-examined 97 published stock market anomalies and found that returns fall by about 26% out of sample relative to the original sample and by 58% after publication, the first part reflecting statistical bias in the original studies and the rest reflecting arbitrage capital moving in once the paper is public. Decay is larger for anomalies that are cheap to trade and in liquid stocks, consistent with traders learning from the literature. Hou, Xue and Zhang (2020) replicated 452 anomalies and found most failed with proper microcap treatment and a t-statistic threshold of 3. The practical base rate: expect a published edge to deliver a third to a half of its paper return, less after costs, and expect it to keep shrinking. Effects with an economic reason, evidence across markets and decades, and modest turnover decay slowest."),
    ("Quantopian's findings on backtest overfitting", "Quantopian ran a platform where hundreds of thousands of users backtested strategies, and its team studied what predicted live performance. Wiecki, Campbell, Lent and Stauth (2016) analyzed 888 algorithms with at least six months of out-of-sample history and found that in-sample Sharpe ratio had almost no predictive value for out-of-sample Sharpe, with an R-squared below 0.025. Features that did carry information were lower in-sample volatility, smaller maximum drawdown, and portfolio construction choices such as hedging. They also found direct evidence of overfitting: the more backtests a user had run on a strategy, the larger the gap between backtest and live results. Quantopian closed in 2020, but its open-source libraries zipline, pyfolio, alphalens and empyrical, and the finding that backtest Sharpe is not a forecast, remain foundational for retail quants."),
    ("Version control for strategies and data", "Strategy code belongs in git with tagged releases so every live trade can be traced to a commit. Data needs its own versioning because vendors restate prices, adjust for corporate actions and fix errors, which silently changes old backtests. Tools such as DVC, LakeFS or plain content-hashed parquet snapshots with a manifest let a backtest cite the exact data it used. Configuration, including universe definitions, cost models and parameters, should be data files under version control rather than constants in code, and every backtest report should print the code commit, data snapshot hash and config hash in its header. Keep a changelog of parameter changes to live strategies with the reason for each, so a later review can separate edge decay from tinkering. Without this it is impossible to know whether the strategy changed or the world did."),
    ("Data cleaning: bad ticks, gaps and outliers", "Raw market data contains erroneous prints, duplicate bars, missing sessions and zero-volume placeholders. A bad tick at ten times the true price creates a fake 900% return that dominates any backtest, and a forward-filled gap creates fake zero returns that shrink volatility. Clean with rules that could have been applied in real time: drop trades outside the exchange session, flag returns beyond a volatility-scaled threshold and confirm against a second source, reject bars where high is below low or close falls outside the range, and take holidays from the exchange calendar rather than inferring them. Never fill gaps with future values. Record every correction so the raw data is preserved. For tick data the Brownlees and Gallo filter, comparing each price to a trimmed local mean, is standard. Cleaning changes results, so run the backtest with and without it and understand the difference."),
    ("Alpha factor evaluation: IC, quantile spreads and turnover", "Before a signal becomes a strategy, evaluate it as a factor. The information coefficient is the Spearman correlation between today's signal ranking and realized forward returns; a daily IC of 0.02 to 0.05 is typical of a usable equity factor, and its mean divided by its standard deviation shows whether it is consistent. Sort the universe into quantiles each period and plot each quantile's return; a good factor is monotonic, with the top-minus-bottom spread positive across subperiods. Measure factor autocorrelation and turnover, because a factor that reshuffles daily needs an IC several times higher to cover transaction costs. Check exposure to size, value and momentum, since a new factor that is 90% momentum brings little diversification. Alphalens automates this analysis. Do it before any portfolio backtest, because it separates signal quality from portfolio construction choices."),
]

